 Here we have $n=2$ and the matrix is 
\[\mathbf{A}=\begin{pmatrix}  -1 & 2 \\  3 i & 4 \end{pmatrix}.\] The entry in the bottom left position is not an integer, so we will have to rationalize the denominator.  Hence,
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  7 & -2  \\  6 &  13 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^4&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  85 & -18  \\  30 &  181 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^6&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^4=\begin{pmatrix}  -259 &   6  \\  90 & 2530 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*} 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^3&=\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  -7 & 14  \\  81 & 124 \end{pmatrix} \\
-3\mathbf{A}&=\begin{pmatrix}  3 & -6  \\  -9 & -12 \end{pmatrix} 
\end{align*}

Consider finding a linear combination of $-\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{A}^2$ that is as close as possible to minimizing the $F_1$ entry of $-\mathbf{A}$.  There are many possible coefficients that are integers:

\begin{align*}
-3\mathbf{A}-13\mathbf{A}^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  47 & -34  \\  -165 &-470  \end{pmatrix} \\
-6\mathbf{A}-19\mathbf{A}^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  61 & -44  \\  -221 & -828 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*} 

Neither of these is as close as we would like, and we are still one step away from $\mathbf{A}^6$.  Trying various other combinations yields

$$22\mathbf{A}+3\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  \ \ \  \ \ 11 & -20  \\  \ \ 131 & \ \ 412 \end{pmatrix}$$ 

This is too close, but only one step away from $\mathbf{A}^6$ and a step closer to "best possible", so this is the solution.  The answer is the ordered pair $(22,3).$ <answer>.

