Note: The intersection $B$ of the normal line with the parabola is found by solving 
\[-\dfrac{1}{2}x + \dfrac{3}{2} = x^2.\] This gives $x^2 + \dfrac{1}{2}x - \dfrac{3}{2} = (x + 1)(x - \dfrac{3}{2}) = 0,$ so $x^2 + \dfrac{1}{2}x - \dfrac{3}{2} = 0.$</remember>

There, $x = -1$ or $x = \dfrac{3}{2}.$ The point where the normal line intersects the parabola for the second time must be $x = \dfrac{3}{2}.$ Plugging this $x$-coordinate into $y = x^2$ gives $y = \left( \dfrac{3}{2} \right)^2 = \dfrac{9}{4},$ so the point $B$ is $\boxed{\left( \dfrac{3}{2}, \dfrac{9}{4} \right)}.$</answer>

